From 03762a1fa7a260d51374d65892601c5977309486 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 18:52:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/14] fix(vendor): portability groundwork for macOS 10.6
 PowerPC

- Root CMakeLists.txt: CMAKE_OBJCXX_STANDARD=23, so the Objective-C++
  engine source (text_shaper/CoreTextLocator.mm) sees the same C++23
  headers as the C++ sources.
- cmake/CheckAtomic.cmake (new): probe whether 64-bit std::atomic needs
  libatomic -- it does on 32-bit PowerPC -- and expose the result as
  contour::atomic, which vtbackend links.
- src/CMakeLists.txt: exactly one `contour` executable may enter the
  build. With CONTOUR_FRONTEND_MACOS the native AppKit frontend
  (src/contour_macos) replaces src/contour wholesale, CLI included.
- crispy/AlignedAllocator.hpp + crispy/CMakeLists.txt: aligned_alloc only
  appeared in macOS's libc in 10.15; probe for it, fall back to
  posix_memalign, then to a manual over-aligned malloc (10.5).
- text_shaper/CMakeLists.txt: link ApplicationServices (CoreText is a
  standalone framework only from 10.8 on); take harfbuzz's include dirs
  from pkg-config instead of the Homebrew-only `brew --prefix` probe,
  which silently expanded to `-I/include` under MacPorts.
- vtpty/Process.hpp: XSI strerror_r (Darwin, the BSDs) returns int and
  fills the buffer; only glibc's GNU variant returns char*.

Rebase note (v0.8.0.9335): the -fexperimental-library hunk this commit
used to carry is gone. Upstream now adds that flag only where a probe
shows std::jthread needs it (libc++ before 20), so GCC/libstdc++ never
sees it. CoreTextLocator.mm is no longer touched here: the 10.6 port of
upstream's 0.8.0 font locator is a patch of its own, later in the series.
